Null pointer exception, or NPE, is a runtime exception that occurs when you try to access or manipulate a null (non-existent) object in your code.

In simple terms, a null pointer exception happens when you try to use a variable that has not been initialized or has not been assigned a value. This can happen when you try to access a null method, property, or field, or when you try to call a method that doesn't exist.

Here's an example in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = null;
        System.out.println(name.length()); // This will throw a NPE
    }
}
```

